# VERDICT: THE FACTORISATION IS DEAD.  r = 1.00 on all 23 cranepack calls that printed, across
# both instances where the operator ran (prob_1 17 calls, prob_20 6), with ncol from 462 to 23,100.
#
# ITS PREMISE IS FALSE, not merely unprofitable.  conflictfactor.md models columns as
# (block, orient, x, y) x (entry window) and rests the whole r^2 saving on r columns sharing one
# (R,U,V).  COLSTAT reports `entries_per_block avg=1.00 max=1` on every call: each block carries
# exactly ONE entry window, so ncol == geom_slots identically and the second factor of the product
# does not exist.  The pre-registered reading was "r < 1.5 -> the factorisation is dead"; measured
# r is 1.00, so the saving is r^2 = 1x.
#
# AND A SECOND, INDEPENDENT REASON.  No COLSTAT line printed at all for prob_3, prob_16, prob_13 or
# prob_2 -- cranepack is never reached on those at a 60 s budget, including prob_3, where brk is
# recorded as paying (80,795 against 96,990 with it off).  Every cost figure this task was built on
# is a 240 s measurement (brk 34-36 s, 22-23% of a worker; ncol 24,318 at 39.1 s).  Either reason
# closes it alone: a 25x speedup of an operator that does not run is worth nothing, and where it
# does run the speedup is 1x.
#
# WHAT SURVIVES.  The note's SECOND lever -- a uniform grid replacing the all-pairs scan within a
# time window, O(N^2) -> O(G x k^2) -- does not depend on r and is untouched by this result.  It is
# new C++ whose gain lands only on the instances where cranepack runs, observed here as 2 of 6.
# Not started: that is a sizing judgement about the remaining time, not a measurement.
